/*Try this out :
- ctrl+f7 toggles the text status bar for general info
- ctrl+alt+d toggles debug mode
- ctrl+alt+c compiles the code
- set a breakpoint on the left grey column with mouse left click
or set a breakpoint with f9 at the cursor
- run the code with f5 and step over each line with f10
- any register can be placed in the top-right watch window
- ctrl+alt+o toggles text op messages in debug mode.
- ctrl+alt+i toggles disassembly in debug mode.
*/
main :: () {
	/*Assembly(asm) instructions take the general form

	op_code operand_0, operand_1, operand_2;

	Op code is necessary but the number of operands depends on the specific op_code.

	Most basic asm instructions take operand 1 and/or 2 as input and 
	place the result in operand 0.
	Operand types can be found in MusicOperandType enum in thegame_data.jai.
	Integers and floats are represented directly.
	Registers are represented by name which can be found in RegDef enum in thegame_data.jai.
	*/
	#asm {
		add ax, 1, 1; //put 1 + 1 into register ax

		//asm instructions do not check for types so
		//the type is set using the post-fix ".f" or ".i"
		//integer instruction is the default without a post-fix
		//So the following 2 instructions are equivalent.
		add.i ax, 2, 2;
		add ax, 2, 2;

		//here we add floating point (real numbers)
		add.f bx, 2.4, 6.1;

		//We can also have only 2 operands such as
		add ax, 1; //put ax + 1 into ax
	}

	/*
	Ram (random access memory) is represented with square brackets [0].
	Ram is accessed by byte 
	[0] = 0th byte
	[1] = 1st byte
	[8] = 8th byte

	Ram can contain registers or integers.
	Ram can have 1 addition such as [ax+1].
	To subtract a value, we add a negative value like [bx + -2] or
	we can place a negative value in a register like ax and then [bx + ax] will subtract ax from bx.

	Integers an floats are all 8 bytes (64 bits).
	Ram should generally be accessed in increments of 8 since all base types are >= 8 bytes here.
	*/
	#asm {
		//Try checking ram(0) in watch window to see the value
		add [0], ax, 3; //put ax + 3 into [0] for 8 bytes since integer is 8 bytes
		add.f [8], bx, 2.1; //put bx + 2.1 into [8] for 8 bytes since float is 8 bytes

		mov [16], [0], 16; //move [16] into [0] for 16 bytes which will move both adds we did above

		//Try out other instructions from OpCode enum in thegame_data.jai
		//and look at their execution code in ExecMInstr in thegame_code.jai
	}

	/*To learn about asm jmps and labels
	load_op cond guides/programming/conditionals
	*/

	//Values can be generally be passed directly into asm blocks as follows :
	i : int = 2;
	#asm {
		sub cx, i, 1; //put i - 1 into cx
	}

	/*Asm does not generally check types and so
	the type needs to match the instruction input.
	*/
	f : float = 3.14;
	#asm {
		//Try flt(cx) in the watch window to view the cx register as a float
		sub.f cx, f, 1.1; //put f - 1.1 into cx
	}
	//Try flt(ram(8)) to see the value of the f variable as a float at [8]

	/*Some assembly instructions to try are add, sub, mul, div, mod, abs...
	All op codes can be found in thegame_data.jai OpCode enum.
	Assembly instructions are executed directly as 
	MusicInstruction struct which can be found in thegame_data.jai.
	MusicInstruction execution is found in thegame_code.jai in function ExecMInstr.
	*/

	/*To learn about branching
	load_op branching guides/programming/branching
	*/

	/*To learn about operators
	load_op operators guides/programming/operators
	*/

	/*To learn music in assembly
	load_op asm_music guides/asm_music
	*/
}